#Rewarding Information, Not Just Elimination

By this point, the environment had gone through several major changes.

I had learned that the action space could make exploration almost impossible.

I had learned that giving the agent too little state information prevented it from adapting to the current search space.

I had also learned that giving it raw candidate data risked exposing too much of the underlying problem.

The Dynamic Split-Ratio Vector became my attempt to give the agent a useful representation of the current decision landscape.

But there was still another major part of the environment to design:

> How should the agent be rewarded?

At first, the obvious answer seemed simple.

The goal of the game is to eliminate candidates until only the correct character remains.

So why not reward the agent based on the number of candidates it removes?

The more candidates eliminated, the better the action.

But when I looked more closely, I realized that **candidate elimination alone was not a good measure of progress**.

# The Problem With Linear Elimination Rewards

Imagine two different situations.

Situation A

The agent starts with 4,200 candidates.

It asks a question and reduces the search space to 4,100.
The agent eliminated:

100 candidates

Now consider another situation.

Situation B

The agent starts with 101 candidates.

It asks a question and reduces the search space to 1.

101 → 1

Again, the agent eliminated:

100 candidates

A simple linear reward would treat both actions as equally valuable.

For example:

reward = candidates_eliminated

Both would receive:

+100

But these decisions are clearly not equivalent.

In the first case:

4,200 → 4,100

the agent has barely changed the problem.

In the second:

101 → 1

the agent has essentially solved it.

This made me realize that the raw number of eliminated candidates does not capture the actual amount of progress.

What mattered was not:

How many candidates disappeared?

but:

How much uncertainty did the agent remove?

Going Back to Information Theory

This brought me back to the same idea that had appeared while building the heuristic baselines.

The Guessing Game is fundamentally a process of reducing uncertainty.

At the beginning of an episode:

There are N possible characters.

The agent does not know which one is the target.

Every useful question should reduce the number of possible states.

A question that cuts the search space in half gives the agent one bit of information.

For example:

100 candidates
      ↓
Ask question
      ↓
50 candidates

The uncertainty has been reduced by a factor of two.

This gave me a much more meaningful way to measure progress.

The Information-Based Reward

The reward now measures the logarithmic reduction of the candidate pool.

The important difference is that the reward is relative.

The second action receives a dramatically larger reward.

Even though both actions eliminated exactly 100 candidates.

This was much closer to what I actually wanted the agent to learn.

A Perfect Split

One of the most useful properties of this reward appears when a question divides the search space exactly in half.

A perfect halving always produces:

+1.0

bit of information.

The same is true regardless of the size of the search space.

4,000 → 2,000  = +1 bit

200 → 100      = +1 bit

20 → 10        = +1 bit

This was one of the reasons I liked the formulation.

The reward measures the quality of the reduction, rather than the absolute scale of the dataset.

Why This Matched the Environment

The game is based on binary questions.

Every question creates two possible branches:

Ideally, it creates something close to:

50% / 50%

A highly unbalanced question might create:

90% / 10%

That question can occasionally produce a large reduction.

But most of the time, it leaves a large portion of the search space behind.

The information-based reward connects the agent's feedback directly to the structure of the search problem.

The better the actual reduction in uncertainty after receiving an answer, the greater the reward.

Rewarding the Result, Not the Heuristic

One thing I wanted to avoid was simply putting the information-gain heuristic directly into the reward, and directly reward the agent based on which pre-calculated score it selected.

That would make the RL objective too close to:

Learn to reproduce the heuristic I already programmed.

Instead, the reward is calculated from the actual transition.

The environment only looks at:

How many candidates existed before?

How many candidates remain after?

The agent is rewarded for what actually happened after interacting with the environment.

This distinction mattered to me.

The heuristic calculates which action should theoretically be useful before asking.

The RL agent chooses an action and learns from the consequences afterward.

There was another problem to consider.

If the agent receives only positive rewards for reducing the search space, it may not have a strong reason to finish efficiently.

A sequence of many small reductions can still accumulate reward.

But the goal is not just:

Reduce uncertainty eventually.

The goal is:

Reduce uncertainty efficiently.

To encourage that, I introduced a small step penalty.

Conceptually:

Every step:
    -0.05

Useful reduction:
    + information reward

So the total incentive becomes something closer to:

Take useful actions

BUT

Do not take unnecessary actions forever.

This creates a trade-off.

A question needs to provide enough value to justify spending another step.

The agent is encouraged to:

avoid useless questions;
avoid extremely slow elimination;
reduce the search space efficiently.

The exact reward values are part of the environment design and may continue changing as I experiment, but the underlying idea became clear:

Progress should be rewarded, but time should still have a cost.

Terminal Rewards

The game also has a clear terminal objective.

Eventually, the agent needs to identify the correct character.

This raises another distinction between:

Good intermediate behaviour

and:

Actually solving the episode

Reducing the search space is useful.

But reducing it is not the final objective by itself.

The environment therefore also uses terminal outcomes to distinguish between:

successfully identifying the target;
incorrect guesses;
and other unsuccessful episode endings.

The reward structure became a combination of:

Information gained
        +
Efficient behaviour
        +
Successful completion
        -
Failure / unnecessary cost

Rather than relying on only one signal.

The Reward Was a Hypothesis Too

The more I worked on this environment, the more I realized that reward design was similar to observation design.

It is not just a technical implementation detail.

A reward function is also a hypothesis.

It answers:

What behaviour do I believe should be encouraged?

A linear elimination reward makes the hypothesis:

Removing more candidates is always proportionally better.

The experiments showed me that this was too simplistic.

The logarithmic reward makes a different hypothesis:

The relative reduction of uncertainty is what matters.

That better matched the structure of the game.

It also connected directly to the earlier heuristic experiments.

The heuristic agents explicitly used information theory to choose actions.

The RL environment now used information reduction to evaluate the consequences of actions.

Connecting the Pieces

By this stage, several parts of the project had started connecting together.

The heuristic agents

Helped establish what efficient information gathering looked like.

The action space failure

Showed that the way decisions are represented can make exploration fail completely.

The observation experiments

Showed that the agent needed access to the current structure of the decision problem without being handed raw candidate identities.

The reward design

Provided a way to measure progress in terms of uncertainty rather than raw candidate counts.

Each piece had emerged from a different problem I encountered while building the project.

None of them were part of the original idea in exactly this form.

What I Was Trying to Learn

At this point, I was no longer simply experimenting with whether DQN could play a guessing game.

The experiment had become more specific.

I wanted to see whether an RL policy, given:

a representation of how available questions partition the current search space;
the ability to choose between those questions;
and rewards based on the amount of uncertainty actually removed;

could learn an efficient information-gathering strategy.

The heuristic agents had already established a strong reference point.

A balanced question should generally be useful.

The RL agent was not explicitly programmed with:

Always choose the split closest to 0.50.

Instead, the environment was designed so that the consequences of efficient information gathering were reflected in its observations and rewards.

Whether the learned policy actually approaches the heuristic behaviour is something that has to be measured experimentally.

That distinction is important.

A good environment design does not guarantee that the agent has learned the intended strategy.

It only creates the conditions under which that strategy can potentially be learned.

The Next Problem: Training Is Not the Same as Evaluation

At this stage, I had:

constrained the action space;
redesigned the observation;
introduced dynamic split ratios;
reshaped the reward around information reduction.

The next step was training.

Initially, I kept the same game constraint I had used for benchmarking:

max_steps = 20

This seemed reasonable.

After all, I wanted an efficient agent.

But during training, I started noticing another problem.

The agent was being cut off before it had enough opportunity to experience successful trajectories.

A limit that made sense for evaluating a trained agent was not necessarily ideal for training an untrained one.

This led to the next distinction I had to make:

The environment used for learning does not always need to be identical to the environment used for evaluation.